## Symptom
During live dogfooding, `hark tts` remained blocked for more than 80 seconds with no audible speech. The operator independently reported hearing nothing. It eventually exited 4 after roughly 120 seconds.

## Captured evidence
`hark logs --json` recorded:
- `tts.error`: `Gemini TTS failed: The read operation timed out`
- provider `google`, voice `eve`
- chars `230`, words `35`
- `latency_ms`: `120487`
- `audio_ms`: `0`
- pid `1402331`

A nearby retry also took 91.175 seconds before succeeding (`tts.ok`, 64.955 seconds audio for 209 chars), so the UX can remain silent for a very long time even without a final error.

## Expected
Bound synthesis/connect/read latency well below 80 seconds; provide prompt progress or a timeout/fallback while no audio is available; always restore audio/mic/queue state on timeout.

## Environment
- Hark CLI reports 0.1.0, editable checkout `/home/xertrov/src/grok/hark`
- checkout description: `v0.1.10-323-g540937d-dirty`
- configured TTS policy: auto with xAI/openAI disabled, selecting Google/Gemini
- no credentials included

## Suggested regression coverage
Simulate a TTS provider read stall and assert a bounded deadline, visible progress/failure, fallback policy, queue release, and mic/media restoration.

Implementation/decision receipt 2026-10-26: 30s synthesis wall deadline spans request upload, framed partial reads/body/EOF and supervisor reap; uses owned cancellation/queue cleanup. Google HTTP bounds 5s connect/20s read; no automatic timeout retry. Independent review and parent review completed; isolated-worktree code merged to master. Final combined remote gate pending.

